:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668361} :answer-field/id #uuid "c423715a-caa3-51ab-b5b9-e65d95fbb26e" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56012d7d-0dc5-5803-80a1-167efecb090a" :question/math-academy-id "q-135307" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “$q$ is a stochastic vector” is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement $Pq=q$ is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.02+0.24 \\\\ 0.18+0.56\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.26 \\\\ 0.74\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$” is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$q$ is a stochastic vector” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8516fd9-972d-5124-9e1f-77abae39069f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer