e/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464508} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5d092e3-b11d-5fd2-902b-f74605931fec" :answer-field/key "$L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465188} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75702486-4350-5a91-a8d6-8dfdbb2ce100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668431} {:answer/id #uuid "de1ee197-65cb-5fa1-9897-8719201beed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de1ee197-65cb-5fa1-9897-8719201beed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668432} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e316fb5-43b4-5727-9714-64602ca9f022" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21a6de96-a543-5af1-a71d-fa101cbb68c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-157392" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,-1,1)$ and $(3,0,-1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{2}=y=\\frac{z+1}{-2}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)-3}{2}\\ne (0)\\ne \\frac{(0)+1}{-2}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(4,-1,0)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n\\frac{(4)-3}{2}\\ne (-1)\\ne \\frac{(0)+1}{-2}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is false. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e283f24-c3a1-59cf-9e3c-55ce9bf0a94a" :