\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$  is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nNow, we consider each step size in turn.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{1.4}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=1.4.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<1.4 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{1.25}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=1.25.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<1.25 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2.5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2.5\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls *outside* the given region, Euler's method is *not* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{0.75}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=0.75.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<0.75 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $Δx=\\frac{1.4}{\\lambda }$; $Δx=\\frac{1.25}{\\lambda }$; $Δx=\\frac{0.75}{\\lambda }$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4bfddbc-eab6-5c68-8480-bef738a56270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466151} {:answer/id #uuid "64147964-eec7-52c1-9f57-1c149a73f852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4bfddbc-eab6-5c68-8480-bef738a56270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466151} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f74ff9e-c2b0-5458-991c-e454a3497dc8" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466919} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dfa3625-eb85-5c68-87b3-150c09ab9de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466153} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1fbef8-4d8a-5576-b0b9-bdc5b60be018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a1fbef8-4d8a-5576-b0b9-bdc5b60be018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466154} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4c8e58a-6367-584c-8e40-5f4fdf246ca5" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466920} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cc80953-1aab-5c6b-9404-d800b2762c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466155} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a54cc8-2891-5cca-bcb5-3ffe47599973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cc80953-1aab-5c6b-9404-d800b2762c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466155} :answer-field/id #uuid "5faeb251-e31b-580b-bd7e-425b6f76dd02" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466921} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cd685e6-acc5-5300-8f25-0e60dc515d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668539} {:answer/id #uuid "29f13f51-d012-500f-a7de-4f350b16e012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cd685e6-acc5-5300-8f25-0e60dc515d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668539} :answer-field/id #uuid "72d5b9cb-fe87-5d34-870a-5cf59bad280a" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d23e4c59-133c-5f47-8ffc-1426beb350c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334199" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation” is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-3(3{e}^{3x})+2({e}^{3x}) & =0 \\\\ 8 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x}-3(2{e}^{2x})+2({e}^{2x}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b82d7e5d-b0de-5b58-bca4-ac8e729c45f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466157} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab340e6-5353-51c7-b5ea-8a8add792b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ab340e6-5353-51c7-b5ea-8a8add792b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466158} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f48045e-f503-5f5e-a726-08ab9c810cd0" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=0.95$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466922} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8e31da1-181d-5e4f-857f-0501ca32b218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466159} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac6bd13-0772-5c18-bfa7-64856f65b7c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ac6bd13-0772-5c18-bfa7-64856f65b7c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466160} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9e161b0-fed7-5810-81f1-2a7e01ce9b27" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=1.2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466923} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e4ceb8f-a388-5519-aadc-917885568bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466161} {:answer/id #uuid "571931ff-cc0a-5f33-897f-276afffae034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "571931ff-cc0a-5f33-897f-276afffae034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466162} :answer-field/id #uuid "d90a7448-b656-5bd0-8615-ca73e640bb2b" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=0.4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466924} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10700099-d2cd-55bb-945c-f724eb06eae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466163} {:answer/id #uuid "40b5a862-ca4b-524f-8192-40e94e1367ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40b5a862-ca4b-524f-8192-40e94e1367ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466164} :answer-field/id #uuid "a55bccd8-2f1f-5b4f-b96a-315698bac866" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=2.3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466925} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7491342c-201b-5bf5-979e-e8b2ed0a7953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668541} {:answer/id #uuid "0459a649-3d87-5152-b9d2-f204c477726d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7491342c-201b-5bf5-979e-e8b2ed0a7953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668541} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a6d9443-2980-5c82-88d1-cf8c0a943119" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9207ec17-19e6-5c10-b901-32c1b663c7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-334255" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the Implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.95,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.95)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.95}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.95}| \\\\  & =0.5128…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.2}| \\\\  & =0.4545…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.4)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.4}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.4}| \\\\  & =0.7142…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.3)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+2.3}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{3.3}| \\\\  & =0.3030…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\lambda Δx=0.95$; $\\lambda Δx=1.2$; $\\lambda Δx=0.4$; $\\lambda Δx=2.3$.\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcf9011c-88d2-522f-b3b2-7f5e58324603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466165} {:answer/id #uuid "7c54ae2f-cbec-5b96-bc9f-83b395505baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcf9011c-88d2-522f-b3b2-7f5e58324603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466165} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b76786e-84b5-5680-8436-15f590623f91" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466926} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e52cab96-26d7-57ef-a7bd-1454284967ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466167} {:answer/id #uuid "08e922da-3532-5a0c-84aa-77682d68a282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08e922da-3532-5a0c-84aa-77682d68a282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466168} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1740d26-3686-55da-8705-422b2c2b00b7" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466927} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b152814-ca69-5689-a07f-ea7a68ee13fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466169} {:answer/id #uuid "800df2b3-aa45-578a-a674-f96d56bef770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b152814-ca69-5689-a07f-ea7a68ee13fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466169} :answer-field/id #uuid "243b5076-4595-54da-af25-5a1ce4f33b19" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466928} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058b97d2-5b6c-5fd2-85ec-1ec9c8532ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668543} {:answer/id #uuid "79b2c63a-c96a-5408-80c6-47f37d11ee73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "058b97d2-5b6c-5fd2-85ec-1ec9c8532ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668543} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9825095-3b77-52eb-b94a-81f7a8fc958a" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4674804-88cb-5499-be8a-d4a228b08c3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334258" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+5y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation” is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-2{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+5{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){e}^{2x} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+5{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e465ed0-28fa-5b9f-95ed-79a40660d4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466171} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf2c758-ce80-5412-81b1-ca972c5051a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecf2c758-ce80-5412-81b1-ca972c5051a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466172} :answer-field/id #uuid "88051d80-7e0f-5259-9d11-0f02843706d0" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466929} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01021e8a-2f30-5b1d-a446-fda33121cb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466173} {:answer/id #uuid "50966b7e-00d2-5249-aaf0-4c04b384797c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50966b7e-00d2-5249-aaf0-4c04b384797c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466174} :answer-field/id #uuid "b80788d5-ebcc-59ed-92b0-24cc521fff81" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466930} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d21b577-109d-5642-8668-cb6bc9643365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466175} {:answer/id #uuid "440e17f7-9b90-5761-8be1-b41c114d1c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d21b577-109d-5642-8668-cb6bc9643365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466175} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b49c89f-2bd2-546f-b4fc-5889ac48750d" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466931} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac59cf60-4567-5ac0-815d-6cbb3c7a55c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668545} {:answer/id #uuid "4f17f9c5-a3c8-509b-ae3b-b23b2a3b1b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac59cf60-4567-5ac0-815d-6cbb3c7a55c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668545} :answer-field/id #uuid "d255ab09-9825-53e0-957b-b6ca841b72bc" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce7557df-4bdb-5791-b8df-2efda5a01a24" :question/math-academy-id "q-334260" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x}+9{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x-1} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x-1}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}+9{e}^{3x-1} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x} & {e}^{3x-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{3x} & 3{e}^{3x-1}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}-3{e}^{3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x-1} \\\\  & =3{e}^{6x-1}-3{e}^{6x-1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b216db04-2d87-5f29-b031-4b8f18aebbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466177} {:answer/id #uuid "084e80ea-fab8-56b6-840f-252ff612f881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "084e80ea-fab8-56b6-840f-252ff612f881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466178} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d31f03f-7baf-56e7-b1ac-efd300ef99ba" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff9dc928-9b30-5b8d-8c18-90d8a6534ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466179} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb19be9-60e0-5255-a502-12dbdfe06395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6eb19be9-60e0-5255-a502-12dbdfe06395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466180} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d0b221-8cd2-5d31-84e5-81ec105281e0" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466933} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22e26dd4-c78f-596b-9c14-f45264b02075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466181} {:answer/id #uuid "c433f3b3-d4f1-587f-a97d-e0085ce49482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c433f3b3-d4f1-587f-a97d-e0085ce49482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466182} :answer-field/id #uuid "98ee50d6-62fb-5851-9ee5-5ce5a71ca168" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466934} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9be53f63-80c4-5335-8318-ed7055a70224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668547} {:answer/id #uuid "9964a441-16f0-5da0-b569-673b8fc47c0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9be53f63-80c4-5335-8318-ed7055a70224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668547} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e625453-8442-5df6-b6cd-303220d221cf" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbddaa01-5158-5d3b-b619-76268088aef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334266" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+4y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 2-3x\\cdot 2x+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3)-3x(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-3{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$” is true. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ 2x & 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$”; “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff45dd62-df17-5749-8a41-f08eebe6dbbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466183} {:answer/id #uuid "7d206c86-5689-5b2c-b54e-7232892b3bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d206c86-5689-5b2c-b54e-7232892b3bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466184} :answer-field/id #uuid "9891fc66-c238-53f0-a9cc-3f49c6b8b39a" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466935} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db3e1dcf-3b31-518b-8484-c581cf7c83d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466185} {:answer/id #uuid "c434960a-d94c-5537-a03f-4ed019706c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c434960a-d94c-5537-a03f-4ed019706c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466186} :answer-field/id #uuid "29753554-0e6b-5e44-9b7f-3c704824a70e" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466936} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6eb2349-e66a-55f0-ab55-a4d8940ad484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466187} {:answer/id #uuid "29697802-a7e8-5fcd-be1a-e444edb2e8bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6eb2349-e66a-55f0-ab55-a4d8940ad484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466187} :answer-field/id #uuid "326f1276-eef3-5446-91bb-9631e7289f2c" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466937} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "243a34a3-876e-5e9a-b44f-428820b9dc61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668549} {:answer/id #uuid "1c4dcd46-fde6-5021-a456-f43166f6de2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "243a34a3-876e-5e9a-b44f-428820b9dc61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668549} :answer-field/id #uuid "06cfd4ff-1bb4-5910-ad1f-556743ac63cb" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a08136e-69ff-52a7-8d77-c9b9c2b3571c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334278" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{